# diff-openbsd set `syscalls` (format: tools/xtask/src/diffopenbsd/scenario.rs): the system
# call probes of tools/difftest, section by section, in one directory of the scratch file
# system (`setup` mounted it on /mnt). Each probe prints its return value or `-1 ENAME`, so a
# different errno is a different line. Probes that hit an unported part of EmiBSD (fifofs,
# exec_script.c, file mappings) have sections of their own, so that their expected
# difference (expected.toml) cannot hide another one. `tty` (N4.7) follows `ostype`, so no
# step id of expected.toml moves: pairs from /dev/ptm, the line discipline, the session's
# terminal, sendsyslog(2) and kern.tty. `tcp` (N6.6a5, D-5) follows `tty`, for the same
# reason: TCP over 127.0.0.1 and ::1, its socket options, TCP_INFO's states, the
# net.inet.tcp values that do not depend on the run, and TCPCTL_DROP of its own connection.
# `udp` (N6.6b0, D-9) follows `tcp`, for the same reason: UDP over 127.0.0.1 and ::1 (bind,
# the names, a datagram each way, the destination address and port as control messages,
# connect's rules, the send errors, a connected socket's ECONNREFUSED from a closed port),
# and net.inet.udp's integers and the statistics' size.
# `raw` (N6.6b1, D-9) follows `udp`: an ICMP echo over 127.0.0.1 with and without IP_HDRINCL,
# the option's answer (the bit, 8), ICMP6_FILTER and IPV6_CHECKSUM's refusals, a divert socket
# reinjecting a UDP datagram out and in with no pf rule, the divert and rip6 sysctls, and the
# raw and divert sockets an unprivileged process is refused (EACCES).
# Before `tcp`, lo0 gets 127.0.0.1/8 (N6.8), as netstart(8) gives it on OpenBSD at boot and as
# nfs.scn does: EmiBSD's ramdisk rc configures no interface, so its bind to 127.0.0.1 was
# EADDRNOTAVAIL; bringing lo0 up also gives it ::1. The step ids before it do not move.

## probes
! mkdir /mnt/sc
! cd /mnt/sc
$ difftest syscalls files
$ difftest syscalls dirs
$ difftest syscalls links
$ difftest syscalls fifo
$ difftest syscalls perms
$ difftest syscalls procs
$ difftest syscalls signals
$ difftest syscalls script
$ difftest syscalls ipc
$ difftest syscalls kqueue
$ difftest syscalls memory
$ difftest syscalls mmapfile
$ difftest syscalls sysctl
$ difftest syscalls ostype
$ difftest syscalls tty
! ifconfig lo0 inet 127.0.0.1/8 up
$ difftest syscalls tcp
$ difftest syscalls udp
$ difftest syscalls raw
$ ls -1a
! cd /

## coredump
! mkdir /mnt/core
! cd /mnt/core
$ sh -c 'ulimit -c unlimited; kill -SEGV $$'; echo status $?; ls -1
! cd /
! rm -rf /mnt/core

## entry
# The system call entry's checks (N4.8b), each in a child with no controlling terminal and
# no core file, its end printed: a call with the stack pointer pivoted off MAP_STACK
# (stackpivot: SIGSEGV), SYS_getpid with bit 32 set in x8 (int-syscall: the number is an int,
# the unpinned site SIGABRT), a plain unpinned SYS_getpid (unpinned-svc: SIGABRT). arm64 only
# (amd64's at N10); it writes no file, so it runs where it is. A set of its own after
# coredump, so no step id of expected.toml moves; this one is syscalls/entry#1.
$ difftest syscalls entry

# The trap classes and the identification registers of the user-mode entry (decision 56, E0),
# recorded before the entry moves: each case in a child, its signal and si_code printed by a
# SA_SIGINFO handler (traps: udf, brk, a misaligned sp, an FP instruction), then the sixteen ID
# register reads OpenBSD's emulate_msr answers, rt = x30 and xzr, the carry flag kept, and the
# DFR0 read and an msr that raise SIGILL (idregs; match flags, never a value). arm64 only. Sets
# of their own after entry: syscalls/entry#2 and #3.
$ difftest syscalls traps
$ difftest syscalls idregs

# The register round trip of the user-mode entry (decision 56, E2's first step), recorded
# before the entry moves: x0-x30, sp, nzcv, tpidr_el0 (set by __set_tcb), v0-v31, fpcr and
# fpsr loaded with a pattern in one asm block and compared after a system call (getppid from
# an svc the binary pins itself), a resolved page fault, a counted loop that timer interrupts
# cross, and a brk whose handler clobbers what it may and returns through sigreturn. A fixed
# header names what each event may change (difftest.c cites the C lines); per case `ok` or the
# first register that differs, never a value. arm64 only. After idregs: syscalls/entry#4.
$ difftest syscalls regs
